---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/Hero.astro';
import ReleaseNotes from '../../components/ReleaseNotes.astro';
import releases from '../../data/homeade-releases.json';
const latest = releases[0];
---
<Base title="HomeADE" description="HomeADE is the project desk for the agents on your computer. It files what comes in, keeps the brief in your notes, and shows which chats, skills, and models are in play." image="/work/homebase.png">
  <Hero variant="graph" seed={13} eyebrow="Agentic development environment" title="HomeADE">
    <p class="lede">HomeADE is the project desk for the agents on your computer. Claude, Codex, and Grok still do the writing, the same tools you would open in a terminal. HomeADE keeps the queue, the notes they read, and a list of every chat, skill, and model on the machine. It runs on its own.</p>
    <a slot="actions" class="btn btn-secondary" href="/homeade/setup">Setup guide</a>
  </Hero>

  <section class="section product">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="product-shots">
        <img class="shot-wide look-pro-only" src="/work/homebase.png" alt="HomeADE on a Mac: a sidebar of screens, and the Agents screen showing how much of each assistant's limit is used and a list of open chats" width="1200" height="814" />
        <img class="shot-wide look-dos-only" src="/work/homebase-dos.png" alt="HomeADE on a Mac in the DOS look: a sidebar of screens, and the Agents screen showing how much of each assistant's limit is used and a list of open chats" width="1200" height="814" />
      </div>

      <p class="card-actions"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://downloads.embry.dev/HomeADE-latest.dmg" download>Download for Mac</a> <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="https://downloads.embry.dev/HomeADE-latest-windows-x64-setup.exe" download>Download for Windows (beta)</a></p>
      <p class="fine">Mac: version {latest.version} &middot; macOS 14 or later &middot; <a href="/homeade/setup">Setup guide</a><br />Windows: version 0.1.0 beta &middot; Windows 10 or later &middot; <a href="/homeade/setup#windows">unsigned</a></p>

      <h2>Run the agents like a project</h2>
      <p>A terminal shows one session. Claude Desktop shows a chat and the files you attach to it. The rest of the work, the open threads, the due dates, the model on each chat, sits in your head. HomeADE lists every chat. You filter, rename, tag, and search them, including the ones you closed. Each row names its model. Today shows what is due. A follow-up an agent schedules stays on that chat and shows on Today and on the calendar.</p>
      <p>Two chats can use two branches of the same repo, so one agent does not overwrite the other's checkout. When this computer is connected to a ticket queue, you start a chat from a ticket in the folder you choose. The ticket, the branch, and the notes stay attached to that chat.</p>

      <h2>Ingest, then file it where the next agent will look</h2>
      <p>Mail, a note you paste, a scan, and a loose file land in Ingest. HomeADE can propose where an item belongs. You confirm the plan, and the note is filed under a project or an area. Send a mail thread into a chat and the agent drafts from the whole conversation. Add to Calendar reads that thread and shows you the event before it writes anything.</p>
      <p>Projects and areas are the second brain. They are ordinary Markdown files in a folder you own. The next chat reads the project note. You do not paste the brief again, and you do not hope a desktop app remembered the right client.</p>

      <h2>Memory you can point at</h2>
      <p>The memory is the notes, the rules, and the chat record. A project note holds the brief. An area note holds the standing context. Setup lists the rules and skills on this machine, so you can read what an agent will follow before you start it. Closed chats stay searchable, with their titles, tags, and models. The decision from last week is a note or a chat you can open, not a scrollback you closed.</p>

      <h2>Skills and models, in one list</h2>
      <p>Settings shows which of Claude, Codex, and Grok are installed, the models each one offers, and the command for any that is missing. You install them and sign in yourself. HomeADE does not keep that login. On a chat you pick the model. For smaller jobs, such as sorting what just arrived, you choose which model does the work. Setup also lists connectors, skills, hooks, rules, scheduled jobs, and commands on this machine. The Agents screen shows how much of each assistant's limit is used, so you can see who still has room.</p>

      <h2>PII stays on the computer you administer</h2>
      <p>There is no HomeADE account. Notes stay in the folder you chose. Chats are stored on this machine. You choose the folder a chat works in. The note reader will not open a note marked private. A prompt goes to Claude, Codex, or Grok only when you run that chat, under the login you set up. You allow a file edit or a command before it runs. API keys stay in the machine's secret store. The <a href="/fieldade/privacy">privacy policy</a> says what leaves the machine.</p>

      <h2>Install it and leave it running</h2>
      <p>Download the disk image, drag HomeADE to Applications, and open it. Apple has signed and notarized the Mac app, so it opens without a warning. An antenna icon sits in the menu bar. Turn on Open at login and it is there each morning. The <a href="/homeade/setup">setup guide</a> covers the install, Claude Code, and the one Terminal step. Nothing else has to be installed.</p>
      <p>The Windows beta runs the same screens in a window, with a tray icon. It is not code-signed yet, so Windows shows a warning.</p>
      <p>FieldADE is an optional iPhone app. HomeADE does not require it.</p>

      <h2 id="releases">Release notes</h2>
      <p>What changed in each version of the Mac app, newest first.</p>
      <ReleaseNotes releases={releases} />

      <p class="back"><a href="/products">All products</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .product h2 { scroll-margin-top: 6.5rem; }
  .product-shots { margin: 0 0 1.5rem; }
  .shot-wide { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 10px; border: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .card-actions { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: .6rem; margin: .5rem 0; }
  .fine { font-size: .85rem; color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .back { margin-top: 2.5rem; }
</style>
